messages:
customer-bob: Hey team — we're seeing streaming responses from the /v1/responses endpoint stall in our Vercel Edge function after ~10-20s. Locally on Node 18 it streams fine, but on edge the ReadableStream stays open and no more chunks arrive. Using @redwood/node-sdk v0.12.3. Any known issues?

support-ash: Thanks for the report — can you share a minimal repro (code + SDK version + runtime)? Also paste the response headers/any request id from the response if possible.

customer-bob: Here's the simplified handler we're using (TypeScript, Vercel Edge):

```ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'
import Redwood from '@redwood/node-sdk'

export default async function handler(req: Request) {
  const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY })
  const res = await client.responses.stream({ input: 'stream me' })
  return new Response(res.body, { headers: { 'content-type': 'text/event-stream' } })
}
```

We don't do any special streaming logic — we just proxy the body through. Logs show status 200 and `transfer-encoding: chunked`. root request id header: rw-request-id: rwd_abc123

devex-liam: A few things to check: 1) edge runtime SDK support changed in v0.13.x (streaming surface got tweaks for ReadableStream). If you're on 0.12.3 you might hit mismatches with newer edge runtime behavior. 2) Are you rotating API keys per chunk? Mid-stream auth rotation can terminate underlying connection. 3) Try upgrading to @redwood/node-sdk@0.13.2 — it fixed several edge streaming GC/backpressure behaviors.

customer-bob: Upgrading now to 0.13.2, will test. One odd log I found: after ~12s stdout shows no new chunks, but the connection stays ESTABLISHED from platform perspective. No 4xx/5xx in the logs.

sre-jen: Another thing — Vercel Edge can aggressively GC or pause microtasks if the function returns quickly. When proxying the ReadableStream you need to make sure the stream is actively consumed by the runtime. Instead of just streaming res.body, try consuming and re-emitting, or use the web stream async iterator to forward chunks and keep the event loop alive. Eg: 

```ts
// consume+forward pattern
const upstream = await client.responses.stream(...)
const reader = upstream.body!.getReader()
return new Response(new ReadableStream({
  async pull(controller) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read()
    if (done) return controller.close()
    controller.enqueue(value)
  }
}), { headers: { 'content-type': 'text/event-stream' } })
```

support-ash: Can you also confirm whether the SDK call returns a ReadableStream or an async iterator for you? In 0.13.x we standardized on returning a web ReadableStream on edge runtimes, but earlier versions had differences depending on host.

customer-bob: After upgrade to 0.13.2 I still see the stall, but I also noticed an earlier 403 in our app logs at ~2s (token refresh triggered), then the token succeeded and subsequent requests accepted. Could that 403 mid-flow be related? We rotate keys per user session — each request generates a fresh short-lived key.

devex-liam: Yes — if you create the stream with one key and then mutate the key on the same backend process, proxies or CDNs might see auth mismatch for subsequent packets. Best practice: obtain a stable key for the lifetime of a single streaming request. If you need per-user rotation, issue a new streaming request after rotation rather than trying to re-auth the same TCP connection.

infra-bot: deploy-bot: Deployment rwd/edge-stream-fix succeeded for commit 9f4a2 (at 1809992300) :rocket:

customer-bob: Trying the consume+forward pattern now and pinning the token per-request. Quick test looks promising — the stream continues past 30s in canary.

support-ash: Great. If you still see intermittent stalls, please capture the rw-request-id and we can check the routed backend logs. Also share whether you're behind any edge middleware (e.g., custom fetch proxies) that might buffer/close idle chunks.

customer-bob: Will do. Thanks all — upgrading SDK + pinning the per-request token and explicit reader loop seems to have resolved it for now :thumbsup:

support-ash: Awesome — please re-open if it recurs and paste the rw-request-id. We'll escalate to infra if needed.
